CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: it argues a position. The position is that the prophets condemn the worshipers and the way they worship, and never the worship the LORD commanded. It has tried to show that from the prophets’ own sentences. Saul is told that to obey is better than sacrifice (1 Samuel 15:22), and the same chapter shows him offering what the LORD had said to destroy. The psalms say the LORD did not desire the offerings and still look to the day when bullocks are offered on his altar (Psalm 51:19). Isaiah names the sacrifices, the new moons and the appointed feasts as the Law’s own and refuses them from hands full of blood, and his remedy is nine commands of conduct (Isaiah 1:10–17). Amos charges Bethel and Gilgal, which Jeroboam devised, and the feasts that “your” hands keep. Hosea and Micah set mercy and the knowledge of God above the offering. Jeremiah stands at the gate of the house and tells the people that the house will go as Shiloh went if they do not amend their ways. Isaiah, Zechariah and Malachi bring the fast, the house and the priests under the same charge. And each of them, in his own hope, names the offerings among the things restored."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What do the prophets say of the offerings?", "They are named as the LORD’s appointment, and refused from these hands", "Isa 1:11–13; Jer 6:20; Amos 5:21–22"),
    ("What is the charge?", "The worshiper’s conduct, his idols and his use of the house", "Isa 1:15–17; Jer 7:9–11; Amos 5:12, 24"),
    ("How are the sentences to be read?", "As the idiom of rank: this more than that, not this and nothing else", "Hos 6:6; Jer 7:22, 23; 1 Sam 15:22"),
    ("What is the sentence on the house?", "Judgment on those who use it, as at Shiloh; not abolition", "Jer 7:12–15; Isa 66:1–4"),
    ("What is the remedy?", "Turn, wash, do justly; and the offerings follow", "Isa 1:16–20; Mic 6:8; Hos 14:1–2"),
    ("What stands in the hope?", "Offerings accepted, priests purified, an offering in righteousness", "Isa 56:7; 66:20, 21; Jer 33:18; Ezek 20:40; Mal 3:3, 4"),
    ("What does Hebrews 10 add?", "The same verdict on their power, and a person; no abolition by the prophets", "Ps 40:6–8; Heb 10:1–10"),
  ], [0.28, 0.44, 0.28]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. It has granted that the prophets’ sentences are strong, that Ezekiel 20:25, 26 is hard, and that Amos 5:25 can be read more than one way. It has named Barton as the strongest voice against it and answered his passages one by one, and it has said where it knows a writer’s position only through a report. Eidevall, Boloje, Groenewald and Lynch agree with its conclusion, and the volume has said where it differs from them in method. If the volume has pressed past an objection unfairly, the verses will show it. The volume has stated no critique of Christian ritual and has made no rule for the Body from the charges it reads."),
]

